course: dictionary
lesson: 11074
title: One word, many meanings
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: Some words have several meanings, and the sentence tells you which one is right. The word welcome is a good example. «Welcome to our school!» is a friendly greeting said to someone who has just arrived, so here welcome means a warm hello.
explain: «We welcome new ideas.» Welcome is now a verb, and it means to be glad to accept something. Ask what would fit: "we are glad to accept new ideas" makes sense, so that is the meaning.
explain: «Thank you for helping me!» «You're welcome.» Here welcome is the polite answer to thank you. It does not mean a greeting at all. Swap in your meaning and check that the sentence still makes sense.
fact: «Welcome» can be a friendly word said to greet someone who arrives.
fact: «Welcome» can be a verb meaning to greet someone warmly, or to be glad to accept something.
fact: «A welcome rest» means a rest that is wanted and pleasant.
fact: «You're welcome» is the polite answer when someone says «thank you».
fact: «Welcome» can also describe someone who is wanted, while an «unwelcome» guest is not wanted.
exercise: «Correcting a teacher privately instead of in front of everyone is kind and is usually welcomed.» What does welcomed mean here? | Greeted at the door | *Accepted gladly | Forgotten quickly | Shouted at loudly
exercise: «Thank you for helping me!» «You're welcome.» What does You're welcome mean? | It is a rude way to say goodbye | It means the helper must leave now | *It is the polite answer to thank you | It asks for a second helping
exercise: «Children are usually welcome at funerals; many say later they were glad they went.» What does welcome mean in this sentence? | Cold and hungry | *Wanted and allowed | Written down in a book | Forced to leave
test: Which meaning fits «a welcome rest»? | *Wanted and pleasant | Rude and short | Lost and afraid | Loud and busy
test: At school, «Welcome to our school!» is said to | Tell the pupils to go home | Ask the time of the lesson | Name the school bell | *Greet someone who has just arrived
exam: The Mole «will always welcome him» in his little home. What does welcome mean here? | To paint the room a new colour | To sell the room to someone else | To stop him from coming in | *To greet him warmly and give him a home
